CPU ownership and x86 handoff regression checks
==============================================

Run from a checkout without building the kernel:

  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_ownership_test.sh
  CC=gcc CFLAGS=-O2 sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_ownership_test.sh
  CC=clang CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all -fno-omit-frame-pointer' \
    sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_ownership_test.sh

The runner extracts verbatim production CPU set and transfer functions, CPU
add/remove senders, generic parked confirmation, and the x86 first-boot,
secondary, hot-add and parked-confirmation paths.
Changes to ownership or publication ordering therefore change the tested code.
Mailbox/firmware execution, allocation, pending waits, instance lookup and lock
scheduling are controlled host boundaries. The separate ack_test.sh exercises
the real pending list, message transport framing and deferred workers.

Before the fix, the first 81 checks reported 25 failures: ownership/tracking
lagged publication, ambiguous handoffs remained assignable, failed or timed-out
requests attempted automatic return, and waiter allocation happened after the
handoff. Expanded coverage includes partial secondary failure, allocation and
mapping failure, confirmed removal and sparse full-width nonrunning transfers.

Review exposed a consumer interaction: x86 host-slot confirmation deletes an
uncertain member, so forward iteration could skip the next running CPU and
falsely permit image overwrite. An additional production-path regression first
failed with success instead of -EBUSY and zero running-CPU probes. Reverse
iteration now checks that CPU and remains safe when the current member is removed.

Ownership is reserved before handoff, and uncertain outcomes remain target-owned
and tracked. This deliberately prefers retention to unsafe pool reuse. A failed
tracking allocation after generic reservation can also retain an unmoved CPU;
this is conservative, not a proof that the CPU entered the instance.

Timeout/cancel removes only a local waiter. It does not cancel queued remote
CPU-start work. Do not use a stopped snapshot or a successful repark alone to
recover an outstanding timed-out add: quiesce such work first. The successful
remove check models an independent acknowledged remove with no outstanding add.

The post-wake allocation case checks ownership/tracking and waiter storage,
not all allocations: real message framing can still allocate and fail after
handoff, covered here by an injected transport error.

These deterministic checks do not prove firmware execution, memory ordering,
resource/image lock serialization, teardown, or actual concurrent CPU hotplug.
Those require kernel runtime and failure-injection tests. This patch alone does
not make the lifecycle or reclamation series ready for publication.
